//! Caravan edge checking -- the three refusals a supervised invocation earns
//! before any capability is exercised, and the order Caravan checks them in.
//!
//! WHY THIS MODULE EXISTS. `refusals.rye` read seL4's own compiled enums, mapped
//! Caravan's four outcomes onto them, and named for each of the seven remaining
//! the subsystem that owes it. Three of those seven are one family, and the
//! agenda said so from the start:
//!
//!   invalid_argument   argument_checking   a supervised method must bound its
//!                                          own arguments before invoking
//!   range_error        bounds_checking     a resource's range belongs in the
//!                                          grant, checked at the edge
//!   alignment_error    alignment_checking  an address handed across the line
//!                                          carries an alignment the object
//!                                          requires
//!
//! They are one family because they are one moment. Each is a question asked of
//! the *request* rather than of the system -- answerable with nothing held but
//! the arguments themselves, before a slot is touched, before a lineage is
//! walked, before a byte of a region is spent. That moment is the **edge**, and
//! this module is it.
//!
//! WHAT GROUNDS IT. seL4 publishes a per-method description for all three, and
//! for `Untyped_Retype` it publishes all three at once -- which makes retyping
//! the honest place to stand rather than an abstraction invented for the rung:
//!
//!   invalid_argument  "The size_bits is too big or too small for the requested
//!                      object type."
//!   range_error       "num_objects is greater than CONFIG_RETYPE_FAN_OUT_LIMIT."
//!   alignment_error   riscv's own page method: "The vaddr is not aligned to the
//!                      page size."
//!
//! THE ORDER IS CARAVAN'S OWN, AND SAYS SO. seL4 lists a method's errors
//! **alphabetically** in its interface XML -- `DeleteFirst`, `FailedLookup`,
//! `IllegalOperation`, `InvalidArgument`, `InvalidCapability`, `NotEnoughMemory`,
//! `RangeError` -- which is a reading order rather than a checking order. No
//! check sequence is published, measured rather than assumed. So the order below
//! is a design judgement, named as one, and it is chosen so that **each check
//! depends only on facts the checks before it established**:
//!
//!   1. argument   Does the request name an object at all, at a size that kind
//!                 can be? Until this holds, the other two questions have no
//!                 subject -- there is no count of a nonexistent object to
//!                 range-check, and no size to align to.
//!   2. range      Does the count stand inside what one invocation may create?
//!                 A count is cheap, independent of any address, and answerable
//!                 the moment the kind is known.
//!   3. alignment  Does the address carry the alignment this object requires?
//!                 Last, because it is the only question needing both the kind's
//!                 size and an address the caller supplies.
//!
//! Exactly one answer comes back, and where a request fails more than one check
//! the **earlier** answer wins. The self-test proves that by building requests
//! that fail two and three at once rather than by asserting it.
//!
//! TWO LINES, BOTH REAL, AND WHICH IS WHICH. seL4 lets one retype create up to
//! `seL4_UntypedRetypeMaxObjects` objects, which the vendored tree states as 256
//! two independent ways. Caravan's own region carves at most
//! `untyped.max_objects`, which is 64. **These are different lines and this
//! module keeps them apart:** the edge answers `seL4_RangeError` at the
//! *kernel's* line, because that is the kernel's own published sentence, and
//! `untyped.rye` refuses by named Rye error at *Caravan's* line, because that is
//! this tree's own capacity. A reader who conflated them would think the kernel
//! forbids what only we do.
//!
//! WHAT THIS PAYS, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT. Two conditions `untyped.rye` refused by
//! named Rye error have published kernel answers, and the edge now speaks them:
//! a `size_bits` outside the untyped limits is seL4's `seL4_InvalidArgument`,
//! and a count past the fan-out limit is its `seL4_RangeError`. `untyped.rye`
//! keeps its own Rye errors unchanged -- they are its capacity, checked where a
//! region is opened. The edge is the boundary; the region is the room. Neither
//! claims the other's ground.
//!
//! CLEAN ROOM. No seL4 line is reproduced. Every size and limit is taken from
//! the vendored BSD-2-Clause headers, and the witness re-reads them at run time
//! together with the three published sentences, so a vendored bump or a reword
//! on either side reds this rung on the lap it lands.
//!
//! Usage:
//!   edge          Run the self-test (default)
//!   edge selftest Same as default
//!   edge table    Print the three checks, their order, and a worked request

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;
const refusals = @import("refusals.rye");
const untyped = @import("untyped.rye");

const caravan_edge_version = "20260821.063720";

/// How many objects one seL4 retype invocation may create, its
/// `seL4_UntypedRetypeMaxObjects`. The vendored tree states 256 two independent
/// ways -- as `KernelRetypeFanOutLimit`'s CMake default and as the fallback in
/// `types.h` when the config macro is absent -- and the witness re-reads both.
///
/// This is **the kernel's line, not Caravan's.** `untyped.max_objects` is 64 and
/// stands for a different reason; the two are deliberately kept apart.
pub const retype_fan_out_limit: u32 = 256;

// =========================================================================
// The three checks, and the order Caravan chose
// =========================================================================

/// The three questions the edge asks, in the order it asks them.
pub const Check = enum(u8) {
    argument = 0,
    range = 1,
    alignment = 2,
};

/// The whole roster, in checking order. One list, so no count is recited and
/// the order lives in exactly one place.
pub const all_checks = [_]Check{ .argument, .range, .alignment };

/// Which kernel refusal this check answers when it fails.
pub fn answers(check: Check) refusals.KernelRefusal {
    // invariant: the switch is total over the roster.
    const answer: refusals.KernelRefusal = switch (check) {
        .argument => .invalid_argument,
        .range => .range_error,
        .alignment => .alignment_error,
    };
    // invariant: an edge check never answers success -- a passing check answers nothing.
    assert(answer != .no_error);
    return answer;
}

/// Which subsystem on the refusal agenda this check pays.
pub fn pays(check: Check) refusals.Owed {
    const owner: refusals.Owed = switch (check) {
        .argument => .argument_checking,
        .range => .bounds_checking,
        .alignment => .alignment_checking,
    };
    // invariant: the check pays exactly the debt its own answer is owed by.
    assert(owner == refusals.owed_by(answers(check)));
    return owner;
}

/// The name each check carries, so a printed table reads as supervision.
pub fn check_name(check: Check) []const u8 {
    const name: []const u8 = switch (check) {
        .argument => "argument",
        .range => "range",
        .alignment => "alignment",
    };
    assert(name.len > 0); // invariant: a named check always carries its name
    return name;
}

/// Why this check stands where it does in the order. The order is Caravan's own,
/// since seL4 publishes its errors alphabetically rather than by check sequence,
/// so the reason travels with the code rather than living only in a comment at
/// the top of the file.
pub fn order_reason(check: Check) []const u8 {
    const reason: []const u8 = switch (check) {
        .argument => "first, because until the request names a real object at a size that kind can be, the other two questions have no subject",
        .range => "second, because a count is answerable the moment the kind is known and needs no address at all",
        .alignment => "last, because it is the only question needing both the kind's size and an address the caller supplies",
    };
    assert(reason.len > 0); // invariant: every position in the order names its reason
    return reason;
}

// =========================================================================
// The request the edge reads
// =========================================================================

/// One retype request, exactly as it arrives at the edge: what to make, how wide
/// each is, how many, and where the first one is asked to land.
pub const Request = struct {
    kind: untyped.ObjectKind = .slot,
    size_bits: u32 = 5,
    count: u32 = 1,
    address: u64 = 0,
};

/// Read a request at the edge, answering the first refusal it earns.
///
/// Answers `.no_error` when every check passes, and otherwise the kernel refusal
/// of the **earliest failing check** in Caravan's stated order. Exactly one
/// answer comes back; a request failing two checks is answered by the earlier.
pub fn read(request: Request) refusals.KernelRefusal {
    for (all_checks) |check| {
        if (!passes(request, check)) {
            const answer = answers(check);
            // invariant: a refused request is answered by one of the kernel's eleven.
            assert(@intFromEnum(answer) < refusals.kernel_refusal_count);
            return answer;
        }
    }
    // invariant: a request passing every check earns no refusal at all.
    assert(clean(request));
    return .no_error;
}

/// Whether this one check passes for this request, asked independently of the
/// others so the order can be proven rather than assumed.
pub fn passes(request: Request, check: Check) bool {
    return switch (check) {
        // seL4: "The size_bits is too big or too small for the requested object
        // type." A kind has exactly one width, and the untyped limits bound what
        // any region can hold, so a size outside either is not a size this
        // object can be.
        .argument => request.size_bits == untyped.object_bits(request.kind) and
            request.size_bits >= untyped.min_untyped_bits and
            request.size_bits <= untyped.max_untyped_bits,

        // seL4: "num_objects is greater than CONFIG_RETYPE_FAN_OUT_LIMIT." A
        // request for no objects is refused here too -- one invocation that
        // creates nothing is a request the kernel's own window arithmetic has no
        // room for, and the kernel's own check reads the window as at least one.
        .range => request.count >= 1 and request.count <= retype_fan_out_limit,

        // riscv seL4: "The vaddr is not aligned to the page size." An object
        // lands on a boundary of its own size, which is the same rule the
        // untyped region holds when it carves -- read here from one place so the
        // edge and the region can never disagree about what aligned means.
        .alignment => untyped.aligned_to(request.address, untyped.object_bits(request.kind)),
    };
}

/// Whether every check passes -- the postcondition `read` proves when it answers
/// success, so a passing answer is never a check quietly skipped.
pub fn clean(request: Request) bool {
    for (all_checks) |check| {
        if (!passes(request, check)) return false;
    }
    return true;
}

/// How many checks this request fails, so the self-test can build requests that
/// fail more than one and prove the earlier answer wins.
pub fn failures(request: Request) u32 {
    var count: u32 = 0;
    for (all_checks) |check| {
        if (!passes(request, check)) count += 1;
    }
    // invariant: a request can fail at most every check there is.
    assert(count <= all_checks.len);
    return count;
}

// =========================================================================
// The checks and a worked request, printed
// =========================================================================

fn print_table() u8 {
    print("edge: version {s}\n", .{caravan_edge_version});
    for (all_checks, 0..) |check, position| {
        print("edge: check {d} {s} answers {s} -- {s}\n", .{
            position + 1,
            check_name(check),
            refusals.kernel_symbol(answers(check)),
            order_reason(check),
        });
    }

    // One request failing all three at once: a page asked for at a slot's width,
    // a count past the kernel's fan-out limit, and an address off every boundary.
    // The earliest answer wins, and the table shows the other two still failing.
    const worst: Request = .{ .kind = .page, .size_bits = 5, .count = retype_fan_out_limit + 1, .address = 1 };
    print("edge: a request failing {d} checks answers {s} -- the earliest, never the loudest\n", .{
        failures(worst),
        refusals.kernel_symbol(read(worst)),
    });

    const good: Request = .{ .kind = .page, .size_bits = 12, .count = 4, .address = 0x8000_0000 };
    print("edge: a request passing all three answers {s}\n", .{refusals.kernel_symbol(read(good))});
    print("edge: the kernel creates at most {d} objects in one invocation; Caravan's own region carves at most {d}\n", .{
        retype_fan_out_limit,
        untyped.max_objects,
    });
    return 0;
}

// =========================================================================
// Self-test -- the asserts are the specification
// =========================================================================

/// Movement one -- each check answers the refusal it owes, and pays the debt the
/// agenda booked against it.
fn prove_agenda() void {
    var seen: u32 = 0;
    for (all_checks, 0..) |check, position| {
        // The roster is in checking order, so a check's position is its own.
        assert(@intFromEnum(check) == position);
        assert(check_name(check).len > 0);
        assert(order_reason(check).len > 0);
        // Each answers a distinct kernel refusal, and pays the matching debt.
        assert(refusals.owed_by(answers(check)) == pays(check));
        seen += 1;
    }
    assert(seen == all_checks.len);

    // The three answers are distinct -- an edge that collapsed two of them would
    // tell a caller to fix the wrong argument.
    assert(answers(.argument) != answers(.range));
    assert(answers(.range) != answers(.alignment));
    assert(answers(.argument) != answers(.alignment));

    // And they are exactly the three debts the agenda calls one family.
    assert(pays(.argument) == .argument_checking);
    assert(pays(.range) == .bounds_checking);
    assert(pays(.alignment) == .alignment_checking);
}

/// Movement two -- each check refuses alone, on a request that fails only it.
/// A check proven only in company is a check that might never have run.
fn prove_each_alone() void {
    // A clean request, which every failing case below is one field away from.
    const good: Request = .{ .kind = .page, .size_bits = 12, .count = 4, .address = 0x8000_0000 };
    assert(clean(good));
    assert(failures(good) == 0);
    assert(read(good) == .no_error);

    // Argument alone: a page asked for at an endpoint's width. The count and the
    // address are untouched, so the other two checks still pass.
    var wrong_size = good;
    wrong_size.size_bits = 4;
    assert(failures(wrong_size) == 1);
    assert(read(wrong_size) == .invalid_argument);
    assert(passes(wrong_size, .range));
    assert(passes(wrong_size, .alignment));

    // Range alone: one object past the kernel's own fan-out limit.
    var too_many = good;
    too_many.count = retype_fan_out_limit + 1;
    assert(failures(too_many) == 1);
    assert(read(too_many) == .range_error);
    assert(passes(too_many, .argument));
    assert(passes(too_many, .alignment));

    // Range alone, the other end: an invocation creating nothing.
    var none = good;
    none.count = 0;
    assert(failures(none) == 1);
    assert(read(none) == .range_error);

    // Alignment alone: a page asked for one byte off a page boundary.
    var askew = good;
    askew.address = 0x8000_0001;
    assert(failures(askew) == 1);
    assert(read(askew) == .alignment_error);
    assert(passes(askew, .argument));
    assert(passes(askew, .range));
}

/// Movement three -- the order is real. Where a request fails more than one
/// check, the earlier answer wins, and the later checks are still genuinely
/// failing rather than accidentally passing.
fn prove_order_holds() void {
    const good: Request = .{ .kind = .page, .size_bits = 12, .count = 4, .address = 0x8000_0000 };

    // Argument and range both fail: argument wins.
    var one_two = good;
    one_two.size_bits = 5;
    one_two.count = retype_fan_out_limit + 1;
    assert(failures(one_two) == 2);
    assert(!passes(one_two, .argument));
    assert(!passes(one_two, .range));
    assert(read(one_two) == .invalid_argument);

    // Range and alignment both fail: range wins.
    var two_three = good;
    two_three.count = 0;
    two_three.address = 0x8000_0007;
    assert(failures(two_three) == 2);
    assert(!passes(two_three, .range));
    assert(!passes(two_three, .alignment));
    assert(read(two_three) == .range_error);

    // Argument and alignment both fail, with range standing between them
    // passing: argument still wins, so the order is not merely "the first
    // failure found by luck of adjacency."
    var one_three = good;
    one_three.size_bits = 4;
    one_three.address = 0x8000_0001;
    assert(failures(one_three) == 2);
    assert(!passes(one_three, .argument));
    assert(passes(one_three, .range));
    assert(!passes(one_three, .alignment));
    assert(read(one_three) == .invalid_argument);

    // All three fail: the earliest wins, never the loudest.
    var all_three = good;
    all_three.kind = .page;
    all_three.size_bits = 5;
    all_three.count = retype_fan_out_limit + 1;
    all_three.address = 1;
    assert(failures(all_three) == 3);
    assert(read(all_three) == .invalid_argument);
}

/// Movement four -- exactly one answer, for every request in a swept space. An
/// edge that could answer twice, or answer nothing, would be no edge at all.
fn prove_totality() void {
    var swept: u32 = 0;
    var clean_seen: u32 = 0;

    for (untyped.all_object_kinds) |kind| {
        var size_bits: u32 = 3;
        while (size_bits <= 13) : (size_bits += 1) {
            var count: u32 = 0;
            while (count <= retype_fan_out_limit + 1) : (count += 61) {
                var address: u64 = 0;
                while (address <= 8192) : (address += 7) {
                    const request: Request = .{
                        .kind = kind,
                        .size_bits = size_bits,
                        .count = count,
                        .address = address,
                    };
                    const answer = read(request);
                    const failed = failures(request);

                    if (failed == 0) {
                        // invariant: a request failing nothing is answered as clean.
                        assert(answer == .no_error);
                        assert(clean(request));
                        clean_seen += 1;
                    } else {
                        // invariant: a request failing anything is refused, never welcomed.
                        assert(answer != .no_error);
                        // invariant: the answer belongs to the earliest failing check.
                        var earliest: Check = .argument;
                        var found = false;
                        for (all_checks) |check| {
                            if (!found and !passes(request, check)) {
                                earliest = check;
                                found = true;
                            }
                        }
                        assert(found);
                        assert(answer == answers(earliest));
                    }
                    swept += 1;
                }
            }
        }
    }

    // invariant: the sweep reached a real space rather than an empty loop.
    assert(swept > 10000);
    // invariant: the space held both welcomed and refused requests, so neither
    // arm of the proof went unexercised.
    assert(clean_seen >= 1);
    assert(clean_seen < swept);
}

/// Movement five -- the seam with the region below. Two conditions `untyped.rye`
/// refuses by named Rye error have published kernel answers, and the edge speaks
/// them without the region changing at all.
///
/// The two lines stay apart on purpose. The kernel creates at most 256 objects
/// in one invocation; Caravan's region carves at most 64. A request for 65 is
/// welcomed by the edge, because the kernel permits it, and refused by the region,
/// because we do not hold that much. Both refusals are honest, and each names its
/// own author.
fn prove_seam() void {
    // A size outside the untyped limits is the kernel's `seL4_InvalidArgument`,
    // and it is what `Region.open` refuses as `RegionTooSmall`.
    const under: Request = .{ .kind = .endpoint, .size_bits = untyped.min_untyped_bits - 1, .count = 1, .address = 0 };
    assert(read(under) == .invalid_argument);
    if (untyped.Region.open(0, untyped.min_untyped_bits - 1)) |_| {
        assert(false); // invariant: the region refuses what the edge names invalid
    } else |err| {
        assert(err == untyped.UntypedError.RegionTooSmall);
    }

    // A count past the kernel's fan-out limit is `seL4_RangeError`.
    const past: Request = .{ .kind = .slot, .size_bits = 5, .count = retype_fan_out_limit + 1, .address = 0 };
    assert(read(past) == .range_error);

    // And the asymmetry, held plainly: 65 objects is fine by the kernel's line
    // and past Caravan's own capacity. The edge welcomes it; the region refuses
    // it by named Rye error. Neither is wrong, and neither speaks for the other.
    const sixty_five: Request = .{ .kind = .slot, .size_bits = 5, .count = untyped.max_objects + 1, .address = 0 };
    assert(sixty_five.count <= retype_fan_out_limit);
    assert(read(sixty_five) == .no_error);

    var region = untyped.Region.open(0, 20) catch {
        assert(false); // invariant: a twenty-bit region stands inside every bound
        unreachable;
    };
    if (region.retype(.slot, untyped.max_objects + 1)) |_| {
        assert(false); // invariant: the region holds its own line whatever the kernel permits
    } else |err| {
        assert(err == untyped.UntypedError.TooManyObjects);
    }

    // The alignment the edge checks is the alignment the region carves to, read
    // from one place, so the two can never disagree about what aligned means.
    assert(untyped.aligned_to(0x8000_0000, untyped.object_bits(.page)));
    const aligned_request: Request = .{ .kind = .page, .size_bits = 12, .count = 1, .address = 0x8000_0000 };
    assert(read(aligned_request) == .no_error);
}

fn run_self_test() u8 {
    prove_agenda();
    prove_each_alone();
    prove_order_holds();
    prove_totality();
    prove_seam();

    // The whole family is answered in the kernel's own vocabulary, taken from the
    // module that reads the eleven out of seL4's header rather than from memory.
    assert(refusals.owed_by(.invalid_argument) == .argument_checking);
    assert(refusals.owed_by(.range_error) == .bounds_checking);
    assert(refusals.owed_by(.alignment_error) == .alignment_checking);

    print("edge: version {s}\n", .{caravan_edge_version});
    print("edge: three questions asked of the request alone, before a slot is touched or a byte is spent.\n", .{});
    print("edge: a size a kind cannot be answers {s}, a count past the kernel's line answers {s}, an address off its boundary answers {s}.\n", .{
        refusals.kernel_symbol(.invalid_argument),
        refusals.kernel_symbol(.range_error),
        refusals.kernel_symbol(.alignment_error),
    });
    print("edge: the order is Caravan's own -- seL4 lists a method's errors alphabetically, so no check sequence is published.\n", .{});
    print("edge: exactly one answer comes back, and where a request fails more than one check the earliest wins.\n", .{});
    print("edge: the kernel creates at most {d} objects in one invocation; Caravan's region carves at most {d} -- two lines, kept apart.\n", .{ retype_fan_out_limit, untyped.max_objects });
    print("edge: three refusals answered, the order proven, exactly one answer swept -- GREEN.\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    const allocator = init.arena.allocator();
    const args = try init.minimal.args.toSlice(allocator);

    if (args.len >= 2 and std.mem.eql(u8, args[1], "table")) return print_table();
    if (args.len >= 2 and std.mem.eql(u8, args[1], "selftest")) return run_self_test();
    if (args.len >= 2) return 1;
    return run_self_test();
}
